/// giter.cdz — a GENERIC (`∀a`) STRICT (eager, fully-materialized) pull-iterator, the element-polymorphic
/// companion to the monomorphic-Int64 `iter.cdz`. This module exists because the recursive-generic PRODUCER element tie
/// (a `List(a) -> GIter(a)` producer whose result element must tie to its argument) was the long-standing
/// inference gap that pinned `iter.cdz` to `Int64`; that gap is now RESOLVED upstream (rcdzc "tie a
/// recursive-generic producer's result element to its argument — Part C, rigid param vars"), verified here
/// by a producer composed with a consumer at TWO distinct element types in one program (Int64 AND String —
/// see `from-list-composes-at-two-element-types` / `collect-instantiates-at-two-types`).
///
/// ⚠ STRICTNESS — this is EAGER, not lazy. The cons-cell is FULLY MATERIALIZED when a producer runs:
/// `from-list`/`range-from` build the ENTIRE `GIter(a)` (a deep recursion) up front, so `take(it, 3)` over a
/// huge source does NOT "pull only 3" — the source is already built (and a source of ≳10⁴ elements overflows
/// the call stack and TRAPS). `next` just projects an ALREADY-BUILT cell. A genuinely LAZY sequence needs a
/// thunk tail (`Cons(a, Unit -> GIter(a))` forced on demand), which this encoding does NOT have (Cadenza is
/// strict). So treat these as EAGER transformers over finite in-memory sequences, NOT lazy streams.
///
/// ENCODING — a recursive CONS-CELL sum (`Nil | Cons(a, GIter(a))`), NOT the reified sum-of-step-shapes
/// `iter.cdz` uses. The two encodings differ on purpose:
///   - `iter.cdz` (mono-Int64) reifies each transformer as a step-shape variant that STORES A CLOSURE over
///     the element (`MapI((Int64 -> Int64), Iter)`, …) — its `next` INTERPRETS one step, so it forces nothing
///     until pulled (that reified form IS lazy without a thunk; the generic cons-cell form here is NOT).
///   - `giter.cdz` (generic) uses the cons-cell recursion because THAT is the shape the Part C tie
///     unblocks: the producer `from-list` recurses through `Cons`, threading the element `h : a` into
///     `Cons(h, from-list(t))`, so the result element ties to the argument element and the whole thing
///     monomorphizes independently per element type. A `FromList(List(a))` wrapper (no self-recursion in
///     the producer) does NOT tie the element and still declines CDZ0201 — verified — so the cons-cell
///     form is the correct generic encoding today.
/// `next` is the pull interface (`GIter(a) -> Option((a, GIter(a)))`): pure (re-stepping yields the same
/// element) and total ON AN ALREADY-BUILT iterator (it never traps itself — but BUILDING a huge source can,
/// per the strictness note above); the cons-cell IS the materialized rest, so `next` just projects it.
///
/// SCOPE (slices 1–18, 21–22; 19–20 deferred — see below). CLOSURE-FREE (Part C shape): producers (`from-list`, `singleton`), `next`, consumers
/// (`count`, `collect`, `nth`, `last`), transformers (`chain`, `take`, `drop`, `zip`, `enumerate`,
/// `intersperse`, `step-by`, `zip3`, `windows2`, `interleave`). CLOSURE-STORING (unblocked by the
/// recursive-generic-transformer closure tie + the rigid-biased if-join): `map`, `filter`, `drop-while`,
/// `take-while`, `scan`, `find`, `last-where`, `all`, `any`, `count-if`, `map-while`, `span-len`,
/// `fold`, `zip-with`, `position` — each threads a closure and monomorphizes per element type. Every function
/// composes at Int64 AND String in one program (the tests pin this).
///
/// DEFERRED (recursive-generic-tie family — v-inference's queued deep slices):
///   - TRANSITIVE-DELEGATE tie: `reduce` (wrapper seeds a 2nd recursive helper with the HEAD element) and
///     `reverse` (…with a bare-Nil ACCUMULATOR) DECLINE at ≥2 element types — an element-typed accumulator
///     threaded THROUGH a delegate is not tied across the wrapper's two monomorphizations (single type
///     compiles+runs; filed + two witnesses routed to v-inference). No annotation workaround for the generic
///     form.
///   - MODULE-SCALE saturation: `repeat-n` (chain n times) and `find-map` (map+find fusion) each compose at
///     ≥2 element types IN ISOLATION, but DECLINE CDZ0201 when added to THIS full module — the accumulated
///     cross-function instantiations exceed what the monomorphizer currently ties (a whole-module effect, not
///     a single-def gap; routed to v-inference as the same deep-tie family surfacing at scale).
/// All land the tick the corresponding tie lands.
/// ELEMENT-CHANGING
/// is supported too: `zip`/`enumerate` yield a tuple element, `map` maps `a -> b` at a DIFFERENT result type
/// (int→Bool, int→String — see the tests), and `zip-with`/`fold` fuse to an inferred result/accumulator type;
/// no `∀`-annotation is needed because the new type is INFERRED from the (unannotated) closure / tuple, never
/// WRITTEN — so the "no-∀-binder-in-annotation" surface gap does not bite here.
///
/// RESOLVED (was a residual inference gap; fixed upstream by rcdzc `565f6d184` "tie an unannotated closure's
/// aggregate-result elements to its domain"):
///   - An UNUSED/identity closure (`fn (s) => s`) at TWO element types now COMPOSES (it previously declined
///     on the closure-body-result-from-domain multi-instantiation residual). Verified: `gmap` with `fn(x)=>x+1`
///     at Int AND `fn(s)=>s` at String in one program runs. So the "closures must USE their parameter" caveat
///     below no longer holds — identity/pass-through closures are fine now.
///
/// KNOWN EDGE this module steers around (NOT Part C, NOT a miscompile):
///   - NULLARY generic producer (`empty : Unit -> GIter(a)`) has NO argument to tie its element to, so a use
///     that does not otherwise constrain `a` (e.g. `count(empty())`) declines CDZ0201 — correct-to-reject
///     (the program is genuinely ambiguous in `a`), though the message misleadingly blames the argument-tie
///     when there is no argument (a diagnostic-wording nit, routed to v-inference). A `from-list([])` whose
///     element IS fixed by context runs correctly. So this module offers no `empty`/`is-empty` yet; a real
///     caller fixes the element in context (`chain(empty(), from-list([1]))`), which lands once `empty` has
///     a determinable element (e.g. an explicit `Type` param) in a follow-up.
///   - MUTUALLY-recursive generic producer GROUP still declines (no shared substitution across the group —
///     v-inference's caveat). Every function here is SELF-recursive, the shape Part C fixed. Do not add a
///     mutually-recursive generic producer pair without re-checking with v-inference.
/// The generic pull-iterator as a recursive cons cell.
///   - `Nil`         — exhausted (the empty iterator).
///   - `Cons(h, rest)` — yield `h : a`, then continue with `rest : GIter(a)`.
type GIter =
  | Nil
  | Cons(a, GIter(a))

/// PRODUCERS ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// `from-list(xs)` — the base generic producer: an iterator over the elements of `xs : List(a)`, in order.
/// SELF-RECURSIVE through `Cons`, so the result element ties to the argument element (the Part C tie) — it
/// composes at more than one element type in a single program (the capability this module demonstrates).
def from-list(xs) = match xs with
  | [] => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => GIter.Cons(h, from-list(t))

/// `singleton(x)` — an iterator yielding exactly `x`, then exhausted. The element is carried by `x`, so
/// `a` is always determined (unlike a nullary `empty()` — see the header's KNOWN GAPS).
def singleton(x) = GIter.Cons(x, GIter.Nil(unit))

/// THE PULL INTERFACE ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Step once: yield `Option((element, rest))` — `None` at exhaustion, never a trap. Total and pure; a
/// re-step of the same `GIter(a)` yields the same element (the cons cell is immutable). The element is `a`;
/// the rest is a `GIter(a)`, so a consumer drives the pull by threading `rest`. (The cons cell already IS
/// the reified rest, so `next` just projects the head and tail.)
def next(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.None(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => Option.Some((h, rest))

/// CONSUMERS (closure-free — the shape Part C directly unblocks) ---------------------------------------
/// `count(it)` — the number of elements the iterator yields. Self-recursive generic consumer: drives the
/// pull to exhaustion, adding one per element. Generic in the element type (it inspects only the shape).
def count(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(_, rest) => 1 + count(rest)

/// `collect(it)` — materialize the remaining elements as a `List(a)`, in pull order. The generic inverse of
/// `from-list` (`collect(from-list(xs)) == xs`). Self-recursive; ties the produced list's element to the
/// iterator's element.
def collect(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => []
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => List.concat([h], collect(rest))

/// `nth(it, n)` — the `n`-th element (0-based), `None` if the iterator is shorter. Closure-free consumer
/// returning `Option(a)`; drives the pull `n` steps. Generic in the element.
def nth(it, n) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.None(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if n <= 0 then Option.Some(h) else nth(rest, n - 1)

/// `last(it)` — the final element, `None` for an empty iterator. Closure-free; recurses to the last cons.
def last(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.None(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => match rest with
    | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.Some(h)
    | GIter.Cons(_, _) => last(rest)

/// CLOSURE-FREE TRANSFORMERS (build a new `GIter(a)`; no closure threaded — the Part C shape) -----------
/// These need NO closure, so they compose per element type. (Closure-threading transformers — `map`/`filter`
/// etc. below — once needed a SEPARATE recursive-generic-transformer closure tie; that LANDED, and the
/// later closure-aggregate-result residual landed too (rcdzc `565f6d184`), so they now compose at ≥2 element
/// types including identity/aggregate-result closures.)
/// `chain(a, b)` — concatenate two iterators: yield all of `a`, then all of `b`. Self-recursive over `a`;
/// the `Nil` base returns `b` (same element type), so the result element ties to the inputs'.
def chain(a, b) = match a with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => b
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(h, chain(rest, b))

/// `take(it, n)` — an iterator over the first `n` elements (fewer if `it` is shorter). Closure-free; builds
/// a truncated `GIter(a)`.
def take(it, n) =
  if n <= 0 then
    GIter.Nil(unit)
  else
    match it with
      | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
      | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(h, take(rest, n - 1))

/// `drop(it, n)` — an iterator with the first `n` elements skipped. Closure-free. Written MATCH-FIRST (not
/// an early `if n <= 0 then it`): returning the bare argument iterator in a guard interacts badly with the
/// element tie and declines CDZ0201, whereas rebuilding the head cell (`Cons(h, rest)`) composes cleanly.
def drop(it, n) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if n <= 0 then GIter.Cons(h, rest) else drop(rest, n - 1)

/// `zip(a, b)` — pair the two iterators element-wise into a `GIter((a, b))`, stopping at the SHORTER one.
/// Closure-free; the result element is a tuple, so this composes at two element types AND changes the
/// element type WITHOUT needing a `∀`-annotation (the tuple element is inferred, not written). A generic
/// zip is the closure-free building block a future `zip-with` (closure-storing) will wrap once the
/// transformer closure-tie lands.
def zip(a, b) = match a with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(ha, ra) => match b with
    | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
    | GIter.Cons(hb, rb) => GIter.Cons((ha, hb), zip(ra, rb))

/// `enum-from(it, i)` — the recursive worker for `enumerate`: pair each element with an index counter that
/// starts at `i` and climbs. Closure-free; the result element is a `(Int64, a)` tuple, so like `zip` it
/// CHANGES the element type without a `∀`-annotation (the tuple is inferred) and composes at ≥2 source
/// element types. `i` is a plain Int64 threaded through the recursion, never fixing `a`.
def enum-from(it, i) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons((i, h), enum-from(rest, i + 1))

/// `enumerate(it)` — pair each element with its 0-based index, yielding a `GIter((Int64, a))`. The
/// iterator analogue of `List.enumerate`; a thin seed of `enum-from` at index 0.
def enumerate(it) = enum-from(it, 0)

/// `intersperse(it, sep)` — insert `sep : a` BETWEEN every pair of adjacent elements (not before the first
/// or after the last), yielding `a, sep, b, sep, c, …`. Closure-free; `sep` shares the element type, so the
/// result element ties to the input's (`GIter(a) -> a -> GIter(a)`) and it composes at ≥2 element types. The
/// inner match on `rest` distinguishes the LAST element (no trailing `sep`) from a middle one; an `n`-element
/// iterator yields `2n-1` elements (0 stays 0, else `2n-1`).
def intersperse(it, sep) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => match rest with
    | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Cons(h, GIter.Nil(unit))
    | GIter.Cons(_, _) => GIter.Cons(h, GIter.Cons(sep, intersperse(rest, sep)))

/// `step-from(it, n, i)` — the recursive worker for `step-by`: yield the current element when the countdown
/// `i` hits 0 (then reset to `n-1`), else skip it and decrement. Closure-free; rebuilds the head cell so the
/// result element ties to the input's, composing at ≥2 element types.
def step-from(it, n, i) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if i <= 0 then
    GIter.Cons(h, step-from(rest, n, n - 1))
  else
    step-from(rest, n, i - 1)

/// `step-by(it, n)` — keep every `n`-th element starting at index 0 (`0, n, 2n, …`), dropping the rest.
/// A thin seed of `step-from` at countdown 0 (so the first element is always kept). `step-by(it, 1)` is the
/// identity; `n <= 0` DEGENERATES to the identity too (the reset countdown `n - 1` never goes positive, so
/// every element yields with the counter stuck ≤ 0 — nothing is skipped, benign, no trap). Callers pass
/// `n >= 1`.
def step-by(it, n) = step-from(it, n, 0)

/// `zip3(a, b, c)` — pair the THREE iterators element-wise into a `GIter((a, b, c))`, stopping at the
/// SHORTEST. Closure-free; the result element is a 3-tuple (element-CHANGING like `zip`), so it composes at
/// ≥2 element types AND across three DIFFERENT element types in one call (String × Int64 × Bool — the
/// tuple is inferred, no `∀`-annotation). The three-way sibling of `zip`.
def zip3(a, b, c) = match a with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(ha, ra) => match b with
    | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
    | GIter.Cons(hb, rb) => match c with
      | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
      | GIter.Cons(hc, rc) => GIter.Cons((ha, hb, hc), zip3(ra, rb, rc))

/// `windows2(it)` — the SLIDING pairs of adjacent elements: `(x0,x1), (x1,x2), …`, yielding a
/// `GIter((a, a))`. Closure-free; the inner match on `rest` peeks the SECOND element (via `h2`) without
/// consuming it (recurses on `rest`, not `rest`'s tail), so each element but the last pairs with its
/// successor. An `n`-element iterator yields `n-1` pairs (0 or 1 element → empty). The width-2 special case
/// of a general `windows(k)`; the tuple element ties both slots to the input element, composing at ≥2 types.
def windows2(it) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => match rest with
    | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
    | GIter.Cons(h2, _) => GIter.Cons((h, h2), windows2(rest))

/// `interleave(a, b)` — alternate elements of the two iterators (`a0, b0, a1, b1, …`); when one runs out,
/// the REMAINDER of the other follows. Closure-free; the `Nil` base returns the other iterator (same element
/// type), and the recursive step SWAPS the two args (`interleave(b, rest)`) so the alternation flips each
/// step. Both inputs share the element type, so the result element ties to theirs — composes at ≥2 types.
def interleave(a, b) = match a with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => b
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(h, interleave(b, rest))

/// CLOSURE-STORING TRANSFORMERS & CONSUMERS (thread a closure through the recursion) -------------------
/// Unblocked by the recursive-generic-transformer closure tie (rcdzc "tie a recursive-generic transformer's
/// closure domain to the mapped element"): a recursive-generic function that threads a closure now
/// monomorphizes per element type, so these compose at ≥2 element types. (An unused/identity closure like
/// `fn (s) => s` USED to decline on a residual gap, now RESOLVED by rcdzc `565f6d184` — identity/pass-through
/// closures compose fine now.)
/// `map(it, f)` — apply `f : a -> b` to every element, yielding a `GIter(b)`. Threads the closure `f`.
/// ELEMENT-CHANGING: `b` may differ from `a` (int→Bool, int→String — see the tests); it is INFERRED from
/// the unannotated closure, so no `∀`-annotation is needed. When `f : a -> a`, this is an in-place map.
def map(it, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(f(h), map(rest, f))

/// `filter(it, p)` — keep the elements satisfying the predicate `p : a -> Bool`. Threads `p`.
def filter(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then GIter.Cons(h, filter(rest, p)) else filter(rest, p)

/// `drop-while(it, p)` — skip the leading run satisfying `p`, then yield the rest unchanged. Threads `p`.
def drop-while(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then drop-while(rest, p) else GIter.Cons(h, rest)

/// `find(it, p)` — the first element satisfying `p`, or `None`. Threads `p`; returns `Option(a)`.
def find(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.None(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then Option.Some(h) else find(rest, p)

/// `last-where(it, p)` — the LAST element satisfying `p`, or `None` (the `find` companion from the other end).
/// Threads `p`; recurses to the tail FIRST, so a later match wins — the post-recursion `Option` fold keeps the
/// deepest `Some`. Returns `Option(a)`; composes at ≥2 element types.
def last-where(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.None(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => match last-where(rest, p) with
    | Option.Some(v) => Option.Some(v)
    | Option.None(_) => if p(h) then Option.Some(h) else Option.None(unit)

/// `pos-from(it, p, i)` — the recursive worker for `position`: the 0-based INDEX (not the element) of the
/// first element satisfying `p`, counting from `i`. Threads `p` and a plain Int64 counter `i` — the counter
/// is NOT element-typed (unlike a `reduce`-style head-seeded accumulator, which trips a transitive tie at ≥2
/// types), so this composes cleanly at ≥2 element types.
def pos-from(it, p, i) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => Option.None(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then Option.Some(i) else pos-from(rest, p, i + 1)

/// `position(it, p)` — the 0-based index of the first element satisfying `p`, or `None`. The index sibling of
/// `find` (which returns the element); a thin seed of `pos-from` at index 0.
def position(it, p) = pos-from(it, p, 0)

/// `all(it, p)` — `true` iff every element satisfies `p` (vacuously true for empty). Threads `p`.
def all(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => true
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then all(rest, p) else false

/// `any(it, p)` — `true` iff some element satisfies `p` (false for empty). Threads `p`.
def any(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => false
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then true else any(rest, p)

/// `count-if(it, p)` — the NUMBER of elements satisfying `p : a -> Bool` (the counting companion of `filter`
/// + `count`, in one pass). Threads `p`; returns an `Int64` (NOT element-typed), so it composes at ≥2
/// element types with no result-element tie needed.
def count-if(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => (if p(h) then 1 else 0) + count-if(rest, p)

/// `map-while(it, f)` — map `f : a -> Option(b)` over the elements, STOPPING at the first `None` (a fused
/// `map` + `take-while` in one closure). Threads `f`; ELEMENT-CHANGING (`b` from the `Some` payload, inferred
/// — int→int, String→int in the tests). The `None` stop branch is a bare `GIter.Nil(unit)` (like
/// `take-while`), tied by the rigid-biased if-join, so it composes at ≥2 element types.
/// (Formerly an identity payload `Some(x)` (`b == a`) tripped a residual closure-body-result gap at ≥2
/// instantiations; RESOLVED by rcdzc `565f6d184` — an identity `Some(x)` payload composes fine now too.)
def map-while(it, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => match f(h) with
    | Option.None(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
    | Option.Some(v) => GIter.Cons(v, map-while(rest, f))

/// `take-while(it, p)` — the longest leading run of elements satisfying `p`, stopping at the first that
/// fails. Threads `p`. Its stop branch is a bare `GIter.Nil(unit)` whose element used to be untied on that
/// path (the nullary-producer edge intersecting the closure tie), so this declined CDZ0201 at ≥2 element
/// types until rcdzc's `rigid-bias the if-branch join` fix: when an `if`'s two branches are the same sum
/// with a single-`Var` element and one side is the rigid param-tied element, the join keeps the rigid one,
/// so the bare-`Nil` else-branch no longer severs the result-element tie. It now composes like the others.
def take-while(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then GIter.Cons(h, take-while(rest, p)) else GIter.Nil(unit)

/// `span-len(it, p)` — the LENGTH of the longest leading run satisfying `p` (`count` of what `take-while`
/// would keep, in one pass, without building the prefix). Threads `p`; returns an `Int64` (NOT element-typed),
/// so it composes at ≥2 element types. `span-len(it, p) + count(drop-while-not…)` = the split point.
def span-len(it, p) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => 0
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => if p(h) then 1 + span-len(rest, p) else 0

/// `scan(it, acc, f)` — a RUNNING fold: emit each intermediate accumulator, starting with the seed `acc`
/// and folding `f : acc -> a -> acc` over the elements. Yields `n+1` values for an `n`-element iterator
/// (the seed, then one per element). Threads BOTH the accumulator and the closure through the recursion;
/// every leaf carries a value-tied element (`acc`, never a bare untied `Nil`), so it composes per element
/// type. `acc` may be a different type than the element `a` (its type is inferred from the seed + `f`).
def scan(it, acc, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Cons(acc, GIter.Nil(unit))
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => GIter.Cons(acc, scan(rest, f(acc, h), f))

/// `fold(it, acc, f)` — a LEFT fold: thread `acc` through the elements, combining with `f : acc -> a -> acc`,
/// and return the final accumulator (the eager reducer `scan` emits the intermediates of). Threads BOTH the
/// accumulator and the closure; `acc` may be any type (inferred from the seed + `f`), so the result is NOT
/// element-typed — `count`/`collect` are its closure-free special cases. Composes at ≥2 source element types
/// via the transformer closure tie. `fold(from-list([1,2,3]), 0, +)` = 6.
def fold(it, acc, f) = match it with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => acc
  | GIter.Cons(h, rest) => fold(rest, f(acc, h), f)

/// `zip-with(a, b, f)` — pair the two iterators element-wise THROUGH a combiner `f : a -> b -> c`, stopping
/// at the SHORTER one; yields a `GIter(c)`. The closure-storing generalization of `zip` (which is
/// `zip-with(a, b, fn(x, y) => (x, y))`): `zip` builds the tuple structurally, `zip-with` lets the caller
/// fuse the pair through `f` so no intermediate tuple is materialized. Threads `f` through the recursion, so
/// it needed the transformer closure tie; the result element `c` is inferred from `f`.
def zip-with(a, b, f) = match a with
  | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
  | GIter.Cons(ha, ra) => match b with
    | GIter.Nil(_) => GIter.Nil(unit)
    | GIter.Cons(hb, rb) => GIter.Cons(f(ha, hb), zip-with(ra, rb, f))

/// TESTS ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Local monomorphic list-equalities (one per element type) — kept monomorphic so the tests exercise the
/// GENERIC producer/consumer composition, not a generic comparison (a generic recursive equality is its own
/// tie question, out of scope for this slice).
def int-list-eq(xs, ys) = match xs with
  | [] => (match ys with
    | [] => true
    | _ => false
  )
  | [x, .. xr] => match ys with
    | [] => false
    | [y, .. yr] => if x == y then int-list-eq(xr, yr) else false

def str-list-eq(xs, ys) = match xs with
  | [] => (match ys with
    | [] => true
    | _ => false
  )
  | [x, .. xr] => match ys with
    | [] => false
    | [y, .. yr] => if x == y then str-list-eq(xr, yr) else false

def bool-list-eq(xs, ys) = match xs with
  | [] => (match ys with
    | [] => true
    | _ => false
  )
  | [x, .. xr] => match ys with
    | [] => false
    | [y, .. yr] => if x == y then bool-list-eq(xr, yr) else false

/// THE headline test: a recursive-generic PRODUCER composed with a generic CONSUMER at TWO distinct
/// element types in ONE program. This exact shape declined with CDZ0201 before the Part C fix.
@test
def from-list-composes-at-two-element-types() =
  if count(from-list([1, 2, 3])) + count(from-list(["a", "bb"])) == 5 then
    unit
  else
    trap("generic producer must compose at Int64 AND String in one program")

@test
def collect-is-inverse-of-from-list-ints() =
  if int-list-eq(collect(from-list([10, 20, 30])), [10, 20, 30]) then
    unit
  else
    trap("collect . from-list == id (Int64)")

@test
def collect-is-inverse-of-from-list-strings() =
  if str-list-eq(collect(from-list(["x", "y"])), ["x", "y"]) then
    unit
  else
    trap("collect . from-list == id (String)")

/// Pins that the SAME generic consumer (`collect`) instantiates at two element types in one program — the
/// consumer companion of the producer-tie headline (both stress per-element-type monomorphization).
@test
def collect-instantiates-at-two-types() =
  if int-list-eq(collect(from-list([1, 2])), [1, 2]) then
    if str-list-eq(collect(from-list(["a"])), ["a"]) then
      unit
    else
      trap("generic consumer at String")
  else
    trap("generic consumer at Int64")

@test
def singleton-yields-one() = if count(singleton("only")) == 1 then unit else trap("singleton count")

/// An empty SOURCE whose element is fixed by the OTHER branch of the comparison — `from-list([1])` in the
/// first arm pins `a = Int64`, so `collect(from-list([]))` here is unambiguous (not the nullary gap).
@test
def empty-int-source-collects-empty() =
  if int-list-eq(collect(from-list([1])), [1]) then
    if count(from-list([2, 3])) == 2 then unit else trap("nonempty count")
  else
    trap("collect singleton int")

@test
def next-yields-head-then-rest() = match next(from-list([42, 43])) with
  | Option.None(_) => trap("next of non-empty must be Some")
  | Option.Some(p) => let (h, rest) = p in
    if h == 42 then if count(rest) == 1 then unit else trap("rest length") else trap("head value")

/// SLICE-2 closure-free combinators, each pinned at TWO element types in one program.
@test
def chain-concatenates-ints() =
